From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jon Pretty <jon.pretty@propensive.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 17:59:46 +0900
Subject: Apply type arguments to a lifted application's expression, not its
 block
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

An application whose arguments must be evaluated once and in order — because a
default argument refers to an earlier argument, a `using` argument among them
— is lifted into a block: the arguments are bound to local values and the
call follows as the block's expression. If the method has a type-parameter
clause after the defaulted clause, Typer then applies the type arguments to
what it has, the block, and `tpd.TypeApply` asserts that it was given a
function: "unexpected tree for type application: Block(…)", a crash in typer.

`appliedToTypeTrees` now applies the type arguments to a block's expression,
inside the block. This is upstream's change 481fe77b7ef ("Allow
`appliedToTypeTrees` to operate on block expressions", #26259), which is on
`main` and every 3.10 release but not on the 3.9 line.

Written with LLM assistance (Claude Code); reviewed content.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Proscala-Patch: blocktypeapply
---
 compiler/src/dotty/tools/dotc/ast/tpd.scala |  4 +++-
 tests/pos/typeapply-lifted-default.scala    | 12 ++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 15 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)
 create mode 100644 tests/pos/typeapply-lifted-default.scala

diff --git a/compiler/src/dotty/tools/dotc/ast/tpd.scala b/compiler/src/dotty/tools/dotc/ast/tpd.scala
index 16b36c940bc4ae317445ac6482b160bce6593a14..7b0dc10cbe8b3b881c20b38220ad22579211aed0 100644
--- a/compiler/src/dotty/tools/dotc/ast/tpd.scala
+++ b/compiler/src/dotty/tools/dotc/ast/tpd.scala
@@ -1028,7 +1028,9 @@ object tpd extends Trees.Instance[Type] with TypedTreeInfo {
 
     /** The current tree applied to given type argument list: `tree[targs(0), ..., targs(targs.length - 1)]` */
     def appliedToTypeTrees(targs: List[Tree])(using Context): Tree =
-      if targs.isEmpty then tree else TypeApply(tree, targs)
+      if targs.isEmpty then tree else tree match
+        case Block(stmts, expr) if stmts.nonEmpty => Block(stmts, TypeApply(expr, targs))
+        case _ => TypeApply(tree, targs)
 
     /** Apply to `()` unless tree's widened type is parameterless */
     def ensureApplied(using Context): Tree =
diff --git a/tests/pos/typeapply-lifted-default.scala b/tests/pos/typeapply-lifted-default.scala
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..842e7de3b6d8342b3787c1d4a7d0380495d72c27
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tests/pos/typeapply-lifted-default.scala
@@ -0,0 +1,12 @@
+// A default argument in a clause before an interleaved type-parameter clause, in an
+// application that is lifted into a block: the type arguments have to be applied to
+// the block's expression, not to the block.
+
+trait TC
+given tc0: TC = new TC {}
+
+def f(using tc: TC)(flags: List[String] = Nil)[C](g: Int => C): C = g(0)
+def h(tc: TC)(flags: List[String] = Nil)[C](g: Int => C): C = g(0)
+
+val viaUsing = f()(identity)
+val viaImpure = h(new TC {})()(identity)
